<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
    </style>

    <!-- The window the app actually runs in, and the window the launch hands
         over TO via postSplashScreenTheme. Its background is what is on screen
         for any moment when neither the system splash nor the WebView is
         drawing, and wherever the WebView does not cover.

         The parent is DayNight, which follows the device. The app does not: its
         palette is dark and only dark, with no light theme anywhere in
         globals.css. So on a phone in light mode the platform supplied a
         near-white window and the app painted a black page inside it, and every
         part of the window the WebView had not covered stayed near-white.

         What was visible was the strip behind the status bar: measured #fafafa
         in light mode and #303030 in dark, against an app background of
         #08070c, so it matched in NEITHER. See #195. The same colour also
         closes the seam between the two splash screens and the moment before
         the first web paint, which cannot be timed away because how long the
         WebView takes to paint is not something this app controls. The answer
         is to make the gap the right colour rather than to make it short.

         windowBackground rather than android:statusBarColor. From API 35 the
         platform enforces edge-to-edge and ignores statusBarColor outright, and
         targetSdkVersion is 36 (variables.gradle). Setting it would be the same
         class of mistake as the launch theme that set android:background on
         Theme.SplashScreen and silently did nothing. The bars are transparent
         already; what shows through them is this. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/appBackground</item>
        <!-- Light GLYPHS are wrong here: these ask for dark icons, and the bars
             now sit on #08070c. False in both, explicitly, rather than relying
             on DayNight to guess from a mode the app does not follow. -->
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
    </style>


    <!-- The launch window, and the only part of startup Android lets an app
         style. From Android 12 the system draws this itself: an icon on a
         colour, with the icon capped at 288dp and any animation capped at
         1000ms. There is no way to put a wordmark here, which is why the
         animated one lives in the WebView (AppSplash.tsx) and this exists only
         to make the seam between them invisible.

         The stock template set android:background to a full-screen
         @drawable/splash PNG. That attribute is not what Theme.SplashScreen
         reads, so on every device running 12 or newer it did nothing at all:
         the app was showing the platform default. The three items below are the
         ones that actually paint.

         postSplashScreenTheme matters as much as the colour: without it the
         window keeps the splash theme after handing over, and the first WebView
         frame draws against the wrong background.

         REPLACING THE ICON. drawable/splash_icon.png is the only file to swap,
         and there are two rules it has to keep, both learned the hard way:

           1. Whatever fills the space around the mark must be exactly the same
              colour as windowSplashScreenBackground, or fully transparent.
              Android masks this drawable to a CIRCLE, so any plate that differs
              from the background renders as a visible disc with its corners cut
              off. The artwork shipped here originally had a dark slate plate on
              a black background and did exactly that.
           2. Keep the mark inside the middle two thirds of the canvas. The
              outer third is what the circular mask eats. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/appBackground</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_icon</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
    </style>
</resources>